### Auth API test requests
### Requires the VS Code "REST Client" extension (humao.rest-client).
### Click "Send Request" above each ### block. Run the dev server first: npm run dev
###
### The access token is returned in the response body, not a cookie. Capture it from a
### register/login/refresh response and paste it into @accessToken below, then re-send.
### The extension keeps cookies automatically within this file for the refresh_token cookie.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000/api/v1
@accessToken = REPLACE_ME
# Must match FRONTEND_ORIGIN in .env: the backend rejects POSTs from any other Origin (CSRF check).
@origin = http://localhost:5173

### 1. Register a member
# @name register
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "testuser@example.com",
  "password": "correcthorsebatterystaple",
  "confirmPassword": "correcthorsebatterystaple",
  "name": "Test User",
  "phone": "9812345678"
}

### 2. Register again with the same email - expect 409 CONFLICT
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "testuser@example.com",
  "password": "correcthorsebatterystaple",
  "confirmPassword": "correcthorsebatterystaple",
  "name": "Test User",
  "phone": "9812345678"
}

### 3. Register with a role field in the body - should still come back as "role": "USER"
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "sneaky@example.com",
  "password": "correcthorsebatterystaple",
  "confirmPassword": "correcthorsebatterystaple",
  "name": "Sneaky User",
  "phone": "9812345680",
  "role": "ADMIN",
  "isAdmin": true
}

### 4. Login as the user from step 1 - copy accessToken from the response into @accessToken above
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "testuser@example.com",
  "password": "correcthorsebatterystaple"
}

### 5. Login with the wrong password - expect 401 UNAUTHENTICATED
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "testuser@example.com",
  "password": "totally-wrong-password"
}

### 6. Login with an email that doesn't exist - expect the SAME 401 message as step 7
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "nobody-here@example.com",
  "password": "whatever-password"
}

### 7. Missing Content-Type header - expect 400 VALIDATION_FAILED
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login

{
  "email": "testuser@example.com",
  "password": "correcthorsebatterystaple"
}

### 8. Who am I? (paste the accessToken from step 6 into @accessToken first)
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### 9. Refresh the access token (rotates the refresh token cookie too)
# @name refresh
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

### 10. Confirm /me still works with the newly refreshed accessToken
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{refresh.response.body.accessToken}}

### 11. Log out
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/logout
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

### 12. Refresh after logout should be 401 UNAUTHENTICATED
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

###
### --- Password reset ---

### 13. Request a reset link - always 204, check the server console for the logged link
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "testuser@example.com"
}

### 13b. Check the link is still usable (204) without using it up - 400 once used or after 15 min
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-reset-token
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "token": "PASTE_TOKEN_FROM_SERVER_CONSOLE"
}

### 14. Reset the password using the token logged to the console
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/reset-password
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "token": "PASTE_TOKEN_FROM_SERVER_CONSOLE",
  "password": "a-brand-new-password"
}

###
### --- Admin ---
### Use the ADMIN_EMAIL / ADMIN_PASSWORD you put in .env when you ran `npm run db:seed`.
### There is no separate admin login endpoint - same /auth/login as everyone else.

### 15. Login as admin (fill in your real admin email/password first)
# @name adminLogin
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "email": "admin@example.com",
  "password": "your-admin-password"
}

### 16. List all users as admin (expect 200 with a users array)
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/users
Authorization: Bearer {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 17. List users with a normal user token (expect 403 FORBIDDEN)
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/users
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
### --- Two-factor authentication (required for the admin) ---
### Needs TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY in .env. Log in as the admin first and paste its accessToken
### into @accessToken at the top.

### 2FA-1. Start setup: returns { secret, otpauthUrl }. In Google Authenticator / Authy / 1Password
### choose "Enter a setup key" and paste `secret` (time-based).
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/2fa/setup
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

{
  "password": "YOUR_ADMIN_PASSWORD"
}

### 2FA-2. Turn it on with the 6-digit code the app shows. SAVE the recoveryCodes it returns
### (shown only once) and paste the new accessToken into @accessToken.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/2fa/enable
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

{
  "code": "123456"
}

### 2FA-3. From now on, login returns { mfaRequired, mfaToken }. Finish with the app's code:
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login/2fa
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}

{
  "mfaToken": "PASTE_mfaToken_FROM_LOGIN",
  "code": "123456"
}

### 2FA-4. Turn 2FA off (code = app code or a recovery code). Admin endpoints stop working until it's back on.
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/2fa/disable
Content-Type: application/json
Origin: {{origin}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

{
  "password": "YOUR_CURRENT_PASSWORD",
  "code": "123456"
}
